#!/bin/bash
# land — merge a PR and put it in production, as ONE action.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS. On 18 Sep 2026 four PRs were merged and nothing was deployed.
# She opened the app to look at a redesign that had landed on `main` an hour
# earlier and saw the old page. Her words: "i dont see a home page redesign" and
# then "how can we fucking fix this so that you merge and deploy at the same
# time". The failure was never permission — merging unprompted was already the
# standing rule. The failure was that merge and deploy were two steps, and the
# second one waited on somebody remembering it.
#
# A rule in CLAUDE.md telling the next agent to remember would be a wish. This is
# the rule as code: you cannot run the merge half without the deploy half.
#
#   land            # the PR for the current branch
#   land 96         # that PR number
#   land 96 --run-e2e  # that PR, and run the full e2e suite on main before production whatever its size
#   land --promote [<repo>] [--run-e2e]   # ship the newest e2e-green main commit to production
#
# A SMALL change ships to production on the fast check alone; a LARGE one (or --run-e2e) runs the
# full e2e suite on main first and ships only on green. "Large" is defined once, in the `large`
# block of this file. A suite already known red on main blocks a small change (E2E_KNOWN_RED).
#
# It refuses rather than guesses: not green, head moved, main red -> it stops and
# says which, and it does NOT deploy. A red main after a merge is the case this
# repo has actually hit (PR #16 in boss-os passed on its head and the merge
# commit went red, already deployed by then), so main is watched to a terminal
# state every single time, not sampled once.
#
# BUILD FIRST, TEST IN BATCHES (26 Sep 2026). A repo whose route names a full e2e workflow
# (E2E_WF) no longer runs that suite per merge: the merge gate is the fast check, and the browser
# suites run nightly + on dispatch. So for those repos `land <pr>` is: fast check green -> merge ->
# main's FAST run green -> deploy STAGING from the merge sha -> production as the plan block decides
# (SMALL CHANGES SHIP ON THE FAST CHECK, below). `land --promote <repo>` ships the newest main
# commit whose e2e run passed and that is newer than what production runs. Production's record is the GitHub Deployments API (environment
# "production", one deployment per land, `gh api repos/../deployments`), written by this script on
# every production deploy of an e2e-routed repo and read back by --promote; nothing is written to
# the git graph. Both deploys run from a clean detached worktree at exactly that sha, never the
# working tree. `--run-e2e` makes --promote dispatch the e2e workflow on main's head and wait for it
# (ceiling 1.5x the last completed run) when no green run is newer than production. A repo whose
# origin/main carries .github/workflows/promote.yml promotes itself on a green e2e: --promote waits
# for any in-flight promote run (bounded) before reading production, never deploys on top of one.
# Repos with no E2E_WF on their route are unchanged: merge -> production, as before.
#
# ON DEMAND ONLY, AND AFTER A LARGE CHANGE (owner, 2 Oct 2026). The e2e workflows no longer run on
# any schedule (`workflow_dispatch` only), so nothing would ever run them after a merge unless a
# person remembered. That is Rule 0 with a person in the loop. So on an E2E_WF route, when the
# merged sha has no green run, `land <pr>` measures the PR (the `large` block below: lines, files,
# a schema or browser-test change, a UI surface touched) and, when it is LARGE, dispatches the e2e
# workflow on main itself, waits for it (bounded), and ships production only on green — printing
# WHY it decided the change was large, and recording that reason on the GitHub Deployment.
# `land <pr> --run-e2e` forces the suite for any size. A red run is a NAMED STOP: staging runs the
# sha, production is untouched.
#
# SMALL CHANGES SHIP ON THE FAST CHECK (owner, 2 Oct 2026, asked and answered). With the suites on
# demand only, a small change used to print WAITING and hold production for a green e2e on its sha
# that nothing would ever run: six repos sat on staging behind CI/docs/test-only changes. Her
# ruling: a SMALL change ships to production on the fast check alone (typecheck, unit, validators);
# e2e gates production only after a LARGE change or when asked (--run-e2e). So on an E2E_WF route:
#   · SMALL  -> main's fast check green -> production from the merge sha, printing and recording
#               "small change: N lines, M files; shipped on the fast check, e2e on demand".
#   · LARGE  -> unchanged: the suite runs on main's head, production only on green.
#   · EVERY CHANGE PRODUCTION HAS NOT SEEN IS JUDGED, not only this PR: each commit between what
#     production runs (its GitHub Deployment record) and the sha about to ship is measured by the
#     same `large` block. One that is large and still unproven (its suite never dispatched, was
#     cancelled, or it was merged without land) makes this land run the suite — a large change
#     never rides out on the small one behind it. Production unreadable, or not an ancestor: large.
#   · KNOWN RED BLOCKS. The newest run of the e2e workflow on main that reached a verdict
#     (cancelled and skipped runs are not verdicts: e2e.yml cancels a superseded dispatch by
#     design) must be success, or there must be none at all. Anything else — failure, timed_out,
#     startup_failure — is a suite KNOWN RED with no green run after it, and a small change is not
#     shipped past it: NAMED STOP [E2E_KNOWN_RED], staging runs the sha, production untouched; fix
#     main and `land <pr> --run-e2e` ships it on a green suite. A red run on the exact sha blocks
#     likewise. A history that cannot be read is not "not red": exit 75, production untouched.
# A self-deploying repo whose production moves through its own workflow (PROMOTE_VIA on the route:
# secondaries, founder-dilution-dashboard, justbeingmercedes) follows the same plan: land confirms
# the staging build, then dispatches that workflow with the sha and the reason; the workflow checks
# the fast check and the known-red rule itself before it moves production, and records the
# GitHub Deployment.
set -uo pipefail

# RUN FROM A COPY. bash reads a script incrementally, so editing this file while a `land` is in
# flight corrupts that run: on 21 Sep 2026 an edit landed mid-run and a live `land 152` died with
# "syntax error near unexpected token `else'" at a line it had not reached when it started. The
# first thing this script does is copy itself somewhere immutable and re-exec from there.
if [ -z "${LAND_RUNNING_FROM_COPY:-}" ]; then
  _copy="$(mktemp -t land.XXXXXX)" || exit 1
  cat "$0" > "$_copy" && chmod +x "$_copy"
  LAND_RUNNING_FROM_COPY=1 exec bash "$_copy" "$@"
fi

say() { printf '\n\033[1m%s\033[0m\n' "$*"; }
die() { printf '\n\033[31mSTOPPED: %s\033[0m\n' "$*"; exit 1; }

MODE="land"; PR=""; TARGET=""; RUN_E2E=""
for a in "$@"; do
  case "$a" in
    --promote) MODE="promote" ;;
    --run-e2e) RUN_E2E=1 ;;
    -h|--help) sed -n '2,40p' "$0" | sed 's/^# \{0,1\}//'; exit 0 ;;
    --*) die "unknown flag $a (land [<pr>] | land --promote [<repo>] [--run-e2e])" ;;
    *) if [ "$MODE" = promote ]; then TARGET="$a"; else PR="$a"; fi ;;
  esac
done
if [ -n "$TARGET" ]; then
  case "$TARGET" in */*|.*) : ;; *) [ -d "$TARGET" ] || TARGET="$HOME/GitHub/$TARGET" ;; esac
  [ -d "$TARGET" ] || die "no such repo directory: $TARGET"
  cd "$TARGET" || die "cannot enter $TARGET"
fi
REPO_DIR="$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null)" || { echo "not in a git repo"; exit 1; }
cd "$REPO_DIR" || exit 1
# A worktree is named after its branch dir, not the repo. Ask git for the
# repo's own name (the common dir) so `land` from a worktree routes correctly.
NAME="$(basename "$(git rev-parse --path-format=absolute --git-common-dir | sed 's#/\.git$##')")"

# The 7403 trap. `npm run deploy:production` in west-peek-os does NOT default the
# account id the way boss-os's script does, so in any shell that lacks it — a
# background job, a fresh login, a launchd context — the very first step fails
# with "The given account is not valid or is not authorized to access this
# service [code: 7403]". That reads like a revoked token and is not one. It cost
# a deploy on 18 Sep 2026: main was green, the merge was done, and production
# silently stayed on the old build. Set it here so no caller has to know.
export CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID="${CLOUDFLARE_ACCOUNT_ID:-8d147e242033699dd37c6f5a451f48d2}"

# --- verify helpers begin (tests/test-land-flow.sh drives the whole script through them) ---
# COULD NOT CHECK IS NOT GREEN, AND IT IS NOT RED EITHER. 26 Sep 2026, in one sweep: how-we-know
# #130's head lookup got "HTTP 401: Requires authentication", this script printed "green, head ,
# MERGEABLE/CLEAN" with an EMPTY head and merged; and horse-legal-guide-velocity #35 got a "TLS
# handshake timeout" on its first call and was refused as "PR #35 is not green" — a green PR left
# unmerged on a network blip. Both are the same defect: an unanswered question read as an answer.
# So every verification read goes through gh_read, which retries a failed read (bounded: 3
# attempts, 2s then 4s), and a read that never answers stops with exit 75 (EX_TEMPFAIL) and the
# words "could not verify", distinct from a real refusal (exit 1), so a caller can try again.
LAND_RC_UNVERIFIED=75
unverified() { printf '\n\033[31mSTOPPED: could not verify (%s) — %s. Nothing was merged by this step; land again when GitHub answers.\033[0m\n' "$1" "$2"; exit "$LAND_RC_UNVERIFIED"; }
# gh_read runs inside $( ), so what went wrong is left in a file, not a variable the subshell loses.
GH_ERR_FILE="$(mktemp -t land-gh-err.XXXXXX)" || exit 1
gh_err() { cat "$GH_ERR_FILE" 2>/dev/null; }
trap 'rm -f "$GH_ERR_FILE"' EXIT
gh_kind() { # stderr text -> network | auth | api
  if grep -qiE 'timeout|timed out|connection (reset|refused)|no such host|dial tcp|EOF|TLS|i/o|network is unreachable|HTTP 50[0-9]|temporarily unavailable' <<<"$1"; then echo network
  elif grep -qiE 'HTTP 401|HTTP 403|authentication|gh auth login|rate limit' <<<"$1"; then echo auth
  else echo api; fi
}
gh_read() { # $1 validation regex for stdout; rest: gh args. Prints stdout; 0 = valid answer, 1 = none after retries
  local want="$1" out err_f GH_ERR try=1 backoff="${LAND_RETRY_SECS:-2}"; shift
  err_f="$(mktemp)"
  while :; do
    out="$(gh "$@" 2>"$err_f")"
    if [ -n "$out" ] && grep -qE "$want" <<<"$out"; then rm -f "$err_f"; printf '%s\n' "$out"; return 0; fi
    GH_ERR="$(head -3 "$err_f" | paste -sd' ' -)"; [ -n "$GH_ERR" ] || GH_ERR="empty answer from gh $1 $2"
    printf '%s' "$GH_ERR" > "$GH_ERR_FILE"
    [ "$try" -ge 3 ] && { rm -f "$err_f"; return 1; }
    echo "     gh $1 $2 did not answer ($GH_ERR) — attempt $try of 3, again in ${backoff}s" >&2
    sleep "$backoff"; try=$((try + 1)); backoff=$((backoff * 2))
  done
}
# --- verify helpers end ---

# How this repo reaches production. westpeek-live deploys itself through its own GitHub workflow
# (DEPLOY_WF) on green main — Workers Builds only uploads it — and land waits for that run; the
# two Worker repos deploy only when a person runs the script.
# Every West Peek web property the "web property change" lane can target MUST have a row here —
# tests/test-land-routes.sh evals this block verbatim and fails if one is missing.
# A row may also set: STAGING (a staging deploy command, run from the merge sha right after
# main's fast check is green), E2E_WF (the name of the full post-merge browser workflow; set,
# a LARGE change waits for a green run of it; a small one ships on the fast check — see the header),
# SMOKE (a URL that must answer 200 with "ok":true after a production deploy), PROMOTE_VIA (a
# self-deploying repo's own workflow that moves production; land dispatches it with `-f sha=` and
# `-f reason=` instead of running a deploy command) and PROMOTE_WF (the repo workflow that ships
# production by itself on a green e2e — waited for, never raced; default promote.yml, and
# PROMOTE_VIA when that is set) and DEPLOY_WF (the repo workflow file that ships production by
# itself on green main, for a route with no E2E_WF — land finds its run on the merge commit, waits
# for it, bounded, and reports its real conclusion; never races it. Default deploy.yml for a route
# with a DEPLOY command; a self-deploying route names it, or tests/test-land-routes.sh fails when
# the repo carries a Cloudflare deploy workflow the route does not wait for).
# --- routes begin (tests/test-land-routes.sh evals this block verbatim) ---
STAGING=""; E2E_WF=""; SMOKE=""; PROMOTE_VIA=""; PROMOTE_WF="promote.yml"; DEPLOY_WF=""
case "$NAME" in
  west-peek-os)         DEPLOY="npm run deploy:production" ; SELF="" ;;
  # 26 Sep 2026 (build first, test in batches): the 217 Playwright journeys left ci.yml for
  # `e2e.yml` (nightly 07:00 UTC + workflow_dispatch), so a merge waits only on the fast CI run and
  # deploy.yml fires on e2e's success and records the GitHub Deployment; `land --promote boss-os
  # [--run-e2e]` ships the newest e2e-green commit from here. No staging target in this repo.
  # 2 Oct 2026: e2e is dispatch-only; `land <pr>` ships a small change on the fast check and runs
  # the suite after a large one — and then WAITS for deploy.yml (PROMOTE_WF) instead of racing it
  # with a second `d1 migrations apply` from the laptop.
  boss-os)              DEPLOY="npm run deploy:production" ; SELF="" ; E2E_WF="e2e" ; PROMOTE_WF="deploy.yml" ;;
  # 6 Oct 2026: Workers Builds is upload-only for west-peek-live. Production ships ONLY through
  # .github/workflows/deploy-cloudflare-worker.yml ("Deploy Cloudflare Worker"): workflow_run on a
  # green "Repository validation" of a push to main, D1 migrations, deploy, postdeploy smoke. Before
  # this row named it, land saw no Cloudflare check-run it understood, printed "nothing to run" and
  # exited 0 before production moved — and said nothing when deploy run 37505035965 went red.
  westpeek-live)        DEPLOY="" ; SELF="deploys itself through its own Deploy Cloudflare Worker workflow on green main (Workers Builds only uploads)" ; DEPLOY_WF="deploy-cloudflare-worker.yml" ; SMOKE="https://westpeek.live/api/runtime/health" ;;
  # The loop repos run in GitHub Actions straight from main: the merge IS the
  # deploy. Every scheduled lane checks out main at fire time.
  how-we-know)          DEPLOY="" ; SELF="runs its lanes from main in GitHub Actions" ;;
  # Cloudflare Pages deploys every push to main; Deploy Distribution (IndexNow/GSC) fires on green.
  sprylabs-hpc-site)    DEPLOY="" ; SELF="deploys itself through Cloudflare Pages on push to main" ;;
